" (https://math.dartmouth.edu/~doyle/docs/walks/walks.pdf)\nciteturn893view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 118; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~doyle/docs/walks/walks.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4575\nL0@P0: Random walks and electric networks\nL1@P0: Peter G. Doyle J. Laurie Snell\nL2@P0: Version dated 5 July 2006\nL3@P0: GNU FDL∗\nL4@P0: Acknowledgement\nL5@P0: This work is derived from the book Random Walks and Electric Net\u0002works, originally published in 1984 by the Mathematical Association of\nL6@P0: America in their Carus Monographs series. We are grateful to the MAA\nL7@P0: for permitting this work to be freely redistributed under the terms of\nL8@P0: the GNU Free Documentation License.\nL9@P0: ∗Copyright (C) 1999, 2000, 2006 Peter G. Doyle and J. Laurie Snell. Derived\nL10@P0: from their Carus Monograph, Copyright (C) 1984 The Mathematical Association\nL11@P0: of America. Permission is granted to copy, distribute and/or modify this document\nL12@P0: under the terms of the GNU Free Documentation License, as published by the Free\nL13@P0: Software Foundation; with no Invariant Sections, no Front-Cover Texts, and no\nL14@P0: Back-Cover Texts.\nL15@P0-1: 1Preface\nL16@P1: Probability theory, like much of mathematics, is indebted to physics as\nL17@P1: a source of problems and intuition for solving these problems. Unfor\u0002tunately, the level of abstraction of current mathematics often makes it\nL18@P1: difficult for anyone but an expert to appreciate this fact. In this work\nL19@P1: we will look at the interplay of physics and mathematics in terms of an\nL20@P1: example where the mathematics involved is at the college level. The\nL21@P1: example is the relation between elementary electric network theory and\nL22@P1: random walks.\nL23@P1: Central to the work will be Polya’s beautiful theorem that a random\nL24@P1: walker on an infinite street network in d-dimensional space is bound to\nL25@P1: return to the starting point when d = 2, but has a positive probability\nL26@P1: of escaping to infinity without returning to the starting point when\nL27@P1: d ≥ 3. Our goal will be to interpret this theorem as a statement about\nL28@P1: electric networks, and then to prove the theorem using techniques from\nL29@P1: classical electrical theory. The techniques referred to go back to Lord\nL30@P1: Rayleigh, who introduced them in connection with an investigation of\nL31@P1: musical instruments. The analog of Polya’s theorem in this connection\nL32@P1: is that wind instruments are possible in our three-dimensional world,\nL33@P1: but are not possible in Flatland (Abbott [1]).\nL34@P1: The connection between random walks and electric networks has\nL35@P1: been recognized for some time (see Kakutani [12], Kemeny, Snell, and\nL36@P1: Knapp [14], and Kelly [13]). As for Rayleigh’s method, the authors\nL37@P1: first learned it from Peter’s father Bill Doyle, who used it to explain a\nL38@P1: mysterious comment in Feller ([5], p. 425, Problem 14). This comment\nL39@P1: suggested that a random walk in two dimensions remains recurrent\nL40@P1: when some of the streets are blocked, and while this is ticklish to prove\nL41@P1: probabilistically, it is an easy consequence of Rayleigh’s method. The\nL42@P1: first person to apply Rayleigh’s method to random walks seems to have\nL43@P1: been Nash-Williams [24]. Earlier, Royden [30] had applied Rayleigh’s\nL44@P1: method to an equivalent problem. However, the true importance of\nL45@P1: Rayleigh’s method for probability theory is only now becoming appre\u0002ciated. See, for example, Griffeath and Liggett [9], Lyons [20], and\nL46@P1: Kesten [16].\nL47@P1: Here’s the plan of the work: In Section 1 we will restrict ourselves\nL48@P1: to the study of random walks on finite networks. Here we will establish\nL49@P1: the connection between the electrical concepts of current and voltage\nL50@P1: and corresponding descriptive quantities of random walks regarded as\nL51@P1: finite state Markov chains. In Section 2 we will consider random walks\nL52@P1-2: 2on infinite networks. Polya’s theorem will be proved using Rayleigh’s\nL53@P2: method, and the proof will be compared with the classical proof using\nL54@P2: probabilistic methods. We will then discuss walks on more general\nL55@P2: infinite graphs, and use Rayleigh’s method to derive certain extensions\nL56@P2: of Polya’s theorem. Certain of the results in Section 2 were obtained\nL57@P2: by Peter Doyle in work on his Ph.D. thesis.\nL58@P2: To read this work, you should have a knowledge of the basic concepts\nL59@P2: of probability theory as well as a little electric network theory and\nL60@P2: linear algebra. An elementary introduction to finite Markov chains as\nL61@P2: presented by Kemeny, Snell, and Thompson [15] would be helpful.\nL62@P2: The work of Snell was carried out while enjoying the hospitality of\nL63@P2: Churchill College and the Cambridge Statistical Laboratory supported\nL64@P2: by an NSF Faculty Development Fellowship. He thanks Professors\nL65@P2: Kendall and Whittle for making this such an enjoyable and rewarding\nL66@P2: visit. Peter Doyle thanks his father for teaching him how to think like a\nL67@P2: physicist. We both thank Peter Ney for assigning the problem in Feller\nL68@P2: that started all this, David Griffeath for suggesting the example to be\nL69@P2: used in our first proof that 3-dimensional random walk is recurrent\nL70@P2: (Section 2.2.9), and Reese Prosser for keeping us going by his friendly\nL71@P2: and helpful hectoring. Special thanks are due Marie Slack, our secre\u0002tary extraordinaire, for typing the original and the excessive number of\nL72@P2: revisions one is led to by computer formatting.\nL73@P2: 1 Random walks on finite networks\nL74@P2: 1.1 Random walks in one dimension\nL75@P2: 1.1.1 A random walk along Madison Avenue\nL76@P2: A random walk, or drunkard’s walk, was one of the first chance pro\u0002cesses studied in probability; this chance process continues to play an\nL77@P2: important role in probability theory and its applications. An example\nL78@P2: of a random walk may be described as follows:\nL79@P2: A man walks along a 5-block stretch of Madison Avenue. He starts\nL80@P2: at corner x and, with probability 1/2, walks one block to the right and,\nL81@P2: with probability 1/2, walks one block to the left; when he comes to\nL82@P2: the next corner he again randomly chooses his direction along Madison\nL83@P2: Avenue. He continues until he reaches corner 5, which is home, or\nL84@P2: corner 0, which is a bar. If he reaches either home or the bar, he stays\nL85@P2: there. (See Figure 1.)\nL86@P2-3: 3Figure 1: ♣\nL87@P3: The problem we pose is to find the probability p(x) that the man,\nL88@P3: starting at corner x, will reach home before reaching the bar. In looking\nL89@P3: at this problem, we will not be so much concerned with the particular\nL90@P3: form of the solution, which turns out to be p(x) = x/5, as with its\nL91@P3: general properties, which we will eventually describe by saying “p(x) is\nL92@P3: the unique solution to a certain Dirichlet problem.”\nL93@P3: 1.1.2 The same problem as a penny matching game\nL94@P3: In another form, the problem is posed in terms of the following game:\nL95@P3: Peter and Paul match pennies; they have a total of 5 pennies; on each\nL96@P3: match, Peter wins one penny from Paul with probability 1/2 and loses\nL97@P3: one with probability 1/2; they play until Peter’s fortune reaches 0 (he\nL98@P3: is ruined) or reaches 5 (he wins all Paul’s money). Now p(x) is the\nL99@P3: probability that Peter wins if he starts with x pennies.\nL100@P3: 1.1.3 The probability of winning: basic properties\nL101@P3: Consider a random walk on the integers 0, 1, 2, . . . , N. Let p(x) be the\nL102@P3: probability, starting at x, of reaching N before 0. We regard p(x) as\nL103@P3: a function defined on the points x = 0, 1, 2, . . . , N. The function p(x)\nL104@P3: has the following properties:\nL105@P3: (a) p(0) = 0.\nL106@P3: (b) p(N) = 1.\nL107@P3: (c) p(x) =\nL108@P3: 1\nL109@P3: 2\nL110@P3: p(x − 1) +\nL111@P3: 1\nL112@P3: 2\nL113@P3: p(x + 1) for x = 1, 2, . . . , N − 1.\nL114@P3: Properties (a) and (b) follow from our convention that 0 and N are\nL115@P3: traps; if the walker reaches one of these positions, he stops there; in\nL116@P3: the game interpretation, the game ends when one player has all of the\nL117@P3-4: 4pennies. Property (c) states that, for an interior point, the probability\nL118@P4: p(x) of reaching home from x is the average of the probabilities p(x−1)\nL119@P4: and p(x + 1) of reaching home from the points that the walker may\nL120@P4: go to from x. We can derive (c) from the following basic fact about\nL121@P4: probability:\nL122@P4: Basic Fact. Let E be any event, and F and G be events such that\nL123@P4: one and only one of the events F or G will occur. Then\nL124@P4: P(E) = P(F) · P(E given F) + P(G) · P(E given G).\nL125@P4: In this case, let E be the event “the walker ends at the bar”, F\nL126@P4: the event “the first step is to the left”, and G the event “the first\nL127@P4: step is to the right”. Then, if the walker starts at x, P(E) = p(x),\nL128@P4: P(F) = P(G) =\nL129@P4: 1\nL130@P4: 2\nL131@P4: , P(E given F) = p(x−1), P(E given G) = p(x+1),\nL132@P4: and (c) follows.\nL133@P4: 1.1.4 An electric network problem: the same problem?\nL134@P4: Let’s consider a second apparently very different problem. We connect\nL135@P4: equal resistors in series and put a unit voltage across the ends as in\nL136@P4: Figure 2.\nL137@P4: Figure 2: ♣\nL138@P4: Voltages v(x) will be established at the points x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. We\nL139@P4: have grounded the point x = 0 so that v(0) = 0. We ask for the voltage\nL140@P4: v(x) at the points x between the resistors. If we have N resistors, we\nL141@P4: make v(0) = 0 and v(N) = 1, so v(x) satisfies properties (a) and (b) of\nL142@P4: Section 1.1.3. We now show that v(x) also satisfies (c).\nL143@P4: By Kirchhoff’s Laws, the current flowing into x must be equal to\nL144@P4: the current flowing out. By Ohm’s Law, if points x and y are connected\nL145@P4-5: 5by a resistance of magnitude R, then the current ixy that flows from x\nL146@P5: to y is equal to\nL147@P5: ixy =\nL148@P5: v(x) − v(y)\nL149@P5: R\nL150@P5: .\nL151@P5: Thus for x = 1, 2, . . . , N − 1,\nL152@P5: v(x − 1) − v(x)\nL153@P5: R\nL154@P5: +\nL155@P5: v(x + 1) − v(x)\nL156@P5: R\nL157@P5: = 0.\nL158@P5: Multiplying through by R and solving for v(x) gives\nL159@P5: v(x) =\nL160@P5: v(x + 1) + v(x − 1)\nL161@P5: 2\nL162@P5: for x = 1, 2, . . . , N − 1. Therefore, v(x) also satisfies property (c).\nL163@P5: We have seen that p(x) and v(x) both satisfy properties (a), (b),\nL164@P5: and (c) of Section 1.1.3. This raises the question: are p(x) and v(x)\nL165@P5: equal? For this simple example, we can easily find v(x) using Ohm’s\nL166@P5: Law, find p(x) using elementary probability, and see that they are the\nL167@P5: same. However, we want to illustrate a principle that will work for very\nL168@P5: general circuits. So instead we shall prove that these two functions are\nL169@P5: the same by showing that there is only one function that satisfies these\nL170@P5: properties, and we shall prove this by a method that will apply to more\nL171@P5: general situations than points connected together in a straight line.\nL172@P5: Exercise 1.1.1 Referring to the random walk along Madison Avenue,\nL173@P5: let X = p(1), Y = p(2), Z = p(3), and W = p(4). Show that properties\nL174@P5: (a), (b), and (c) of Section 1.1.3 determine a set of four linear equations\nL175@P5: with variables X, Y , Z and W. Show that these equations have a\nL176@P5: unique solution. What does this say about p(x) and v(x) for this special\nL177@P5: case?\nL178@P5: Exercise 1.1.2 Assume that our walker has a tendency to drift in one\nL179@P5: direction: more specifically, assume that each step is to the right with\nL180@P5: probability p or to the left with probability q = 1 − p. Show that\nL181@P5: properties (a), (b), and (c) of Section 1.1.3 should be replaced by\nL182@P5: (a) p(0) = 0.\nL183@P5: (b) p(N) = 1.\nL184@P5: (c) p(x) = q · p(x − 1) + p · p(x + 1).\nL185@P5-6: 6Exercise 1.1.3 In our electric network problem, assume that the re\u0002sistors are not necessarily equal. Let Rx be the resistance between x\nL186@P6: and x + 1. Show that\nL187@P6: v(x) =\nL188@P6: 1\nL189@P6: Rx−1\nL190@P6: 1\nL191@P6: Rx−1\nL192@P6: +\nL193@P6: 1\nL194@P6: Rx\nL195@P6: v(x − 1) +\nL196@P6: 1\nL197@P6: Rx\nL198@P6: 1\nL199@P6: Rx−1\nL200@P6: +\nL201@P6: 1\nL202@P6: Rx\nL203@P6: v(x + 1).\nL204@P6: How should the resistors be chosen to correspond to the random walk\nL205@P6: of Exercise 1.1.2?\nL206@P6: 1.1.5 Harmonic functions in one dimension; the Uniqueness\nL207@P6: Principle\nL208@P6: Let S be the set of points S = {0, 1, 2, . . . , N}. We call the points of\nL209@P6: the set D = {1, 2, . . . , N − 1} the interior points of S and those of\nL210@P6: B = {0, N} the boundary points of S. A function f(x) defined on S is\nL211@P6: harmonic if, at points of D, it satisfies the averaging property\nL212@P6: f(x) =\nL213@P6: f(x − 1) + f(x + 1)\nL214@P6: 2\nL215@P6: .\nL216@P6: As we have seen, p(x) and v(x) are harmonic functions on S having\nL217@P6: the same values on the boundary: p(0) = v(0) = 0; p(N) = v(N) =\nL218@P6: 1. Thus both p(x) and v(x) solve the problem of finding a harmonic\nL219@P6: function having these boundary values. Now the problem of finding\nL220@P6: a harmonic function given its boundary values is called the Dirichlet\nL221@P6: problem, and the Uniqueness Principle for the Dirichlet problem asserts\nL222@P6: that there cannot be two different harmonic functions having the same\nL223@P6: boundary values. In particular, it follows that p(x) and v(x) are really\nL224@P6: the same function, and this is what we have been hoping to show. Thus\nL225@P6: the fact that p(x) = v(x) is an aspect of a general fact about harmonic\nL226@P6: functions.\nL227@P6: We will approach the Uniqueness Principle by way of the Maxi\u0002mum Principle for harmonic functions, which bears the same relation\nL228@P6: to the Uniqueness Principle as Rolle’s Theorem does to the Mean Value\nL229@P6: Theorem of Calculus.\nL230@P6: Maximum Principle . A harmonic function f(x) defined on S\nL231@P6: takes on its maximum value M and its minimum value m on the bound\u0002ary.\nL232@P6: Proof. Let M be the largest value of f. Then if f(x) = M for x\nL233@P6: in D, the same must be true for f(x − 1) and f(x + 1) since f(x) is\nL234@P6: the average of these two values. If x − 1 is still an interior point, the\nL235@P6-7: 7same argument implies that f(x − 2) = M; continuing in this way, we\nL236@P7: eventually conclude that f(0) = M. That same argument works for\nL237@P7: the minimum value m. ♦\nL238@P7: Uniqueness Principle. If f(x) and g(x) are harmonic functions\nL239@P7: on S such that f(x) = g(x) on B, then f(x) = g(x) for all x.\nL240@P7: Proof. Let h(x) = f(x) − g(x). Then if x is any interior point,\nL241@P7: h(x − 1) + h(x + 1)\nL242@P7: 2\nL243@P7: =\nL244@P7: f(x − 1) + f(x + 1)\nL245@P7: 2\nL246@P7: −\nL247@P7: g(x − 1) + g(x + 1)\nL248@P7: 2\nL249@P7: ,\nL250@P7: and h is harmonic. But h(x) = 0 for x in B, and hence, by the Max\u0002imum Principle, the maximum and mininium values of h are 0. Thus\nL251@P7: h(x) = 0 for all x, and f(x) = g(x) for all x. ♦\nL252@P7: Thus we finally prove that p(x) = v(x); but what does v(x) equal?\nL253@P7: The Uniqueness Principle shows us a way to find a concrete answer:\nL254@P7: just guess. For if we can find any harmonic function f(x) having the\nL255@P7: right boundary values, the Uniqueness Principle guarantees that\nL256@P7: p(x) = v(x) = f(x).\nL257@P7: The simplest function to try for f(x) would be a linear function; this\nL258@P7: leads to the solution f(x) = x/N. Note that f(0) = 0 and f(N) = 1\nL259@P7: and\nL260@P7: f(x − 1) + f(x + 1)\nL261@P7: 2\nL262@P7: =\nL263@P7: x − 1 + x + 1\nL264@P7: 2N\nL265@P7: =\nL266@P7: x\nL267@P7: N\nL268@P7: = f(x).\nL269@P7: Therefore f(x) = p(x) = v(x) = x/N.\nL270@P7: As another application of the Uniqueness Principle, we prove that\nL271@P7: our walker will eventually reach 0 or N. Choose a starting point x with\nL272@P7: 0 < x < N. Let h(x) be the probability that the walker never reaches\nL273@P7: the boundary B = {0, N}. Then\nL274@P7: h(x) =\nL275@P7: 1\nL276@P7: 2\nL277@P7: h(x + 1) +\nL278@P7: 1\nL279@P7: 2\nL280@P7: h(x − 1)\nL281@P7: and h is harmonic. Also h(0) = h(N) = 0; thus, by the Maximum\nL282@P7: Principle, h(x) = 0 for all x.\nL283@P7: Exercise 1.1.4 Show that you can choose A and B so that the func\u0002tion f(x) = A(q/p)\nL284@P7: x + B satisfies the modified properties (a), (b) and\nL285@P7: (c) of Exercise 1.1.2. Does this show that f(x) = p(x)?\nL286@P7: Exercise 1.1.5 Let m(x) be the expected number of steps, starting at\nL287@P7: x, required to reach 0 or N for the first time. It can be proven that\nL288@P7: m(x) is finite. Show that m(x) satisfies the conditions\nL289@P7-8: 8(a) m(0) = 0.\nL290@P8: (b) m(N) = 0.\nL291@P8: (c) m(x) =\nL292@P8: 1\nL293@P8: 2m(x + 1) +\nL294@P8: 1\nL295@P8: 2m(x − 1) + 1.\nL296@P8: Exercise 1.1.6 Show that the conditions in Exercise 1.1.5 have a unique\nL297@P8: solution. Hint: show that if m and m¯ are two solutions, then f = m−m¯\nL298@P8: is harmonic with f(0) = f(N) = 0 and hence f(x) = 0 for all x.\nL299@P8: Exercise 1.1.7 Show that you can choose A, B, and C such that\nL300@P8: f(x) = A+Bx+Cx\nL301@P8: 2\nL302@P8: satisfies all the conditions of Exercise 1.1.5. Does\nL303@P8: this show that f(x) = m(x) for this choice of A, B, and C?\nL304@P8: Exercise 1.1.8 Find the expected duration of the walk down Madison\nL305@P8: Avenue as a function of the walker’s starting point (1, 2, 3, or 4).\nL306@P8: 1.1.6 The solution as a fair game (martingale)\nL307@P8: Let us return to our interpretation of a random walk as Peter’s fortune\nL308@P8: in a game of penny matching with Paul. On each match, Peter wins\nL309@P8: one penny with probability 1/2 and loses one penny with probability\nL310@P8: 1/2. Thus, when Peter has k pennies his expected fortune after the\nL311@P8: next play is\nL312@P8: 1\nL313@P8: 2\nL314@P8: (k − 1) +\nL315@P8: 1\nL316@P8: 2\nL317@P8: (k + 1) = k,\nL318@P8: so his expected fortune after the next play is equal to his present for\u0002tune. This says that he is playing a fair game; a chance process that can\nL319@P8: be interpreted as a player’s fortune in a fair game is called a martingale.\nL320@P8: Now assume that Peter and Paul have a total of N pennies. Let\nL321@P8: p(x) be the probability that, when Peter has x pennies, he will end up\nL322@P8: with all N pennies. Then Peter’s expected final fortune in this game is\nL323@P8: (1 − p(x)) · 0 + p(x) · N = p(x) · N.\nL324@P8: If we could be sure that a fair game remains fair to the end of the\nL325@P8: game, then we could conclude that Peter’s expected final fortune is\nL326@P8: equal to his starting fortune x, i.e., x = p(x) · N. This would give\nL327@P8: p(x) = x/N and we would have found the probability that Peter wins\nL328@P8: using the fact that a fair game remains fair to the end. Note that the\nL329@P8: time the game ends is a random time, namely, the time that the walk\nL330@P8: first reaches 0 or N for the first time. Thus the question is, is the\nL331@P8: fairness of a game preserved when we stop at a random time?\nL332@P8-9: 9Unfortunately, this is not always the case. To begin with, if Peter\nL333@P9: somehow has knowledge of what the future holds in store for him, he\nL334@P9: can decide to quit when he gets to the end of a winning streak. But\nL335@P9: even if we restrict ourselves to stopping rules where the decision to\nL336@P9: stop or continue is independent of future events, fairness may not be\nL337@P9: preserved. For example, assume that Peter is allowed to go into debt\nL338@P9: and can play as long as he wants to. He starts with 0 pennies and\nL339@P9: decides to play until his fortune is 1 and then quit. We shall see that\nL340@P9: a random walk on the set of all integers, starting at 0, will reach the\nL341@P9: point 1 if we wait long enough. Hence, Peter will end up one penny\nL342@P9: ahead by this system of stopping.\nL343@P9: However, there are certain conditions under which we can guarantee\nL344@P9: that a fair game remains fair when stopped at a random time. For our\nL345@P9: purposes, the following standard result of martingale theory will do:\nL346@P9: Martingale Stopping Theorem. A fair game that is stopped at\nL347@P9: a random time will remain fair to the end of the game if it is assumed\nL348@P9: that there is a finite amount of money in the world and a player must\nL349@P9: stop if he wins all this money or goes into debt by this amount.\nL350@P9: This theorem would justify the above argument to obtain p(x) =\nL351@P9: x/N.\nL352@P9: Let’s step back and see how this martingale argument worked. We\nL353@P9: began with a harmonic function, the function f(x) = x, and inter\u0002preted it as the player’s fortune in a fair game. We then considered the\nL354@P9: player’s expected final fortune in this game. This was another harmonic\nL355@P9: function having the same boundary values and we appealed to the Mar\u0002tingale Stopping Theorem to argue that this function must be the same\nL356@P9: as the original function. This allowed us to write down an expression\nL357@P9: for the probability of winning, which was what we were looking for.\nL358@P9: Lurking behind this argument is a general principle: If we are given\nL359@P9: boundary values of a function, we can come up with a harmonic function\nL360@P9: having these boundary values by assigning to each point the player’s\nL361@P9: expected final fortune in a game where the player starts from the given\nL362@P9: point and carries out a random walk until he reaches a boundary point,\nL363@P9: where he receives the specified payoff. Furthermore, the Martingale\nL364@P9: Stopping Theorern allows us to conclude that there can be no other\nL365@P9: harmonic function with these boundary values. Thus martingale theory\nL366@P9: allows us to establish existence and uniqueness of solutions to a Dirich\u0002let problem. All this isn’t very exciting for the cases we’ve been con\u0002sidering, but the nice thing is that the same arguments carry through\nL367@P9: to the more general situations that we will be considering later on.\nL368@P9-10: 10The study of martingales was originated by Levy [19] and Ville\nL369@P10: [34]. Kakutani [12] showed the connection between random walks and\nL370@P10: harmonic functions. Doob [4] developed martingale stopping theorems\nL371@P10: and showed how to exploit the preservation of fairness to solve a wide\nL372@P10: variety of problems in probability theory. An informal discussion of\nL373@P10: martingales may be found in Snell [32].\nL374@P10: Exercise 1.1.9 Consider a random walk with a drift; that is, there is\nL375@P10: a probability p 6=\nL376@P10: 1\nL377@P10: 2\nL378@P10: of going one step to the right and a probability\nL379@P10: q = 1 − p of going one step to the left. (See Exercise 1.1.2.) Let\nL380@P10: w(x) = (q/p)\nL381@P10: x\nL382@P10: ; show that, if you interpret w(x) as your fortune when\nL383@P10: you are at x, the resulting game is fair. Then use the Martingale\nL384@P10: Stopping Theorem to argue that\nL385@P10: w(x) = p(x)w(N) + (1 − p(x))w(0).\nL386@P10: Solve for p(x) to obtain\nL387@P10: p(x) =\nL388@P10: \u0010\nL389@P10: q\nL390@P10: p\nL391@P10: \u0011x\nL392@P10: − 1\nL393@P10: \u0010\nL394@P10: q\nL395@P10: p\nL396@P10: \u0011N\nL397@P10: − 1\nL398@P10: .\nL399@P10: Exercise 1.1.10 You are gambling against a professional gambler; you\nL400@P10: start with A dollars and the gambler with B dollars; you play a game\nL401@P10: in which you win one dollar with probability p <\nL402@P10: 1\nL403@P10: 2\nL404@P10: and lose one dollar\nL405@P10: with probability q = 1−p; play continues until you or the gambler runs\nL406@P10: out of money. Let RA be the probability that you are ruined. Use the\nL407@P10: result of Exercise 1.1.9 to show that\nL408@P10: RA =\nL409@P10: 1 −\nL410@P10: \u0010\nL411@P10: p\nL412@P10: q\nL413@P10: \u0011B\nL414@P10: 1 −\nL415@P10: \u0010\nL416@P10: p\nL417@P10: q\nL418@P10: \u0011N\n"
